course: dictionary
lesson: 6749
title: Words that sound like their meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like the noise they describe. Say them out loud and you can almost hear the sound: buzz, hiss, crash, pop. This kind of word is called onomatopoeia, spelled O-N-O-M-A-T-O-P-O-E-I-A. Say it in parts: on-o-mat-o-pee-a.
explain: Read this: «The bacon sizzled in the pan.» You can hear the bacon in the word sizzled. Words like this work like a sound effect in a film, so the writing feels lively and noisy.
explain: Onomatopoeia is not the same as alliteration. Alliteration repeats the first sound of words close together, like «slithering snakes slide silently». Onomatopoeia is a word that sounds like what it means, such as buzz, bang or splash. The test is simple: does the word sound like its noise?
explain: Animal sounds change from country to country. An English dog says «woof», a French dog says «ouaf», and a Ukrainian dog says «hav». The dog is the same, but each language has its own sound words.
fact: Onomatopoeia means words that sound like what they mean, such as buzz, bang and hiss.
fact: An onomatopoeia is one such word, for example «pop» or «crash».
fact: Comics use onomatopoeia in big letters, like POW!, CRASH! and BOOM!
fact: Animal sounds differ between languages: «woof» in English, «ouaf» in French and «hav» in Ukrainian.
fact: Onomatopoeia comes from Greek words meaning "making names", and it is spelled O-N-O-M-A-T-O-P-O-E-I-A.
exercise: Which word sounds like the noise it names? | table | *crash | quickly | happy
exercise: «The bacon sizzled in the pan.» Find and copy the onomatopoeic word. | *sizzled | bacon | pan | the
exercise: Ella says alliteration and onomatopoeia are the same. Which is correct? | Onomatopoeia is a noise a dog makes | They are the same thing | *They are different: alliteration repeats first sounds, onomatopoeia sounds like its meaning | Alliteration repeats the first sounds of words, and that is the same as onomatopoeia
test: Which sound word would an English dog say? | hav | ouaf | moo | *woof
test: In the sentence «The balloon went pop», which word sounds like its meaning? | balloon | the | *pop | went
exam: Which statement about onomatopoeia is true? | Onomatopoeia repeats the first sound of words close together | *Onomatopoeia is a word that sounds like the noise it describes, such as buzz or hiss | Onomatopoeia is only found in maths lessons | Onomatopoeia is the name of a country
